33. Dependencia Theories and the Neoclassical Counterrevolution
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: IV | Topic: Dependencia theories of development; the neoclassical counterrevolution
Introduction
Two rival schools shaped development thinking. Dependency theory, rooted in Latin America and Marxism, blames external structures for underdevelopment. The neoclassical counterrevolution blames state failure and calls for markets.
Dependencia theories
Core claim: underdevelopment is not an early stage but a product of the capitalist world system, in which the periphery is exploited by the core.
1. Prebisch-Singer thesis: terms of trade of primary exports decline relative to manufactures, so peripheral countries lose from specialisation in primary goods. Led to import substitution industrialisation (ECLA).
2. Neo-Marxist school (Paul Baran, A.G. Frank): "development of underdevelopment": metropole-satellite chains transfer surplus from periphery to core. Capitalism holds peripheral countries in dependence.
3. Dependent development (Cardoso, Faletto, Evans): some industrialisation is possible in the periphery, but remains dependent on foreign capital and technology and is skewed in distribution.
4. World-systems theory (Wallerstein): core, semi-periphery and periphery in a single division of labour. Unequal exchange (Emmanuel, Amin).
5. False-paradigm model (Todaro): advice from developed-country experts is inappropriate.
Policy: delink or reduce dependence, import substitution, state ownership, regional integration, redistribution.
Critique: East Asia grew through integration with global markets; theory ignores domestic policy failures and elites; ISI led to inefficiency and debt.
The neoclassical counterrevolution
From the 1980s (Reagan, Thatcher, IMF/World Bank), economists such as Bauer, Lal, Balassa, Krueger and Little argued that underdevelopment results from poor resource allocation due to excessive state intervention.
• Free-market analysis: prices should be right; liberalise trade, privatise, deregulate, stabilise.
• Public-choice: governments are rent-seeking and corrupt (Krueger's "rent-seeking society").
• Market-friendly approach (World Bank 1991): state should provide stable macro environment, invest in people and infrastructure.
• Washington Consensus (Williamson): fiscal discipline, tax reform, trade and financial liberalisation, FDI, privatisation, property rights.
Critique: structural adjustment imposed social costs; market failures in developing countries are real; East Asian successes involved targeted interventions; consensus stressed institutions later (post-Washington Consensus, Stiglitz).
Conclusion
Neither extreme explains development fully. The modern synthesis recognises both market failures and government failures, with emphasis on institutions and context.